I recently read the postings about how to mount /usr remotely on OSF.
Since this is something that I do on Ultrix, and something that is key
to my environment I read these articles with great interest.

I just received a 3000/500X and a 3000/300. By following the directions
I was able to remotely mount /usr on the 3000/300 from that 3000/500X,
although I did have the problem with the sia command that was mentioned.

One question that was brought up in the postings was what to do about
routed, since the one in /usr/sbin obviously isn't available until
after /usr is mounted, which can't happen if the server machine is on
a different subnet (assuming you don't want to play around with explicit
route commands). I was able to find a solution to this.

There is a statically linked version of routed on the distribution CDROM
that shows up in /bin in the System Management file system. After bringing
up your system normally, remount the CDROM, and your disk root filesystem
from the System Management environment, and then copy /sbin/routed to
your disk root filesystem. I did this, rebooted, and everything seemed
to be fine.

I've heard from reliable sources that DEC will NOT be including a supported
dataless environment in OSF 1.3 so it's important that the method for doing
this become common knowledge and even get included in the OSF FAQ

