Well, here we go again :-)  This discussion just took place over in
misc.forsale.computer.workstaiton and in some of the sun groups.

Here's some price/performance information.  I'm using list prices,
because even though I can get educational discounts, not everyone can.
I chose the machines from the previous list that most closely match
the SS10 in performance.

machine    SPECint92  SPECfp92  SPECmark89  List $   note:
----------------------------------------------------------------
SS10/30    44.2       52.9      57.3        16,000   32M, 19"C, disk
HP 715/50  37.1       72.8      69.0        15,600   32M, 19"C, disk
SGI IndR4  57.6       60.3      70.2        18,000   32M, 19"C, disk
SGI IndyPC 35         34                    11,000   32M, 19"C, disk
SGI IndySC 60         58                    15,500   32M, 19"C, disk
DEC3k300L  45.9       63.6                   8,700   32M, 16"C, 1g disk
DEC3k300   66.2       91.5                  13,000   32M, 19"c, 1g disk

Notes: Except for the DEC machines, all have 4-500 meg disks.  I know
that everyonce can buy these machines at less than list, but list
price is a well-known data point.  F'rinstance, the SGI machines
include outrageously priced memory upgrades ($1500 / 16Mb), which one
could get elsewhere for 1/3 the price.  But I went with straight list
for everyone.  DEC will not sell you a 19" color monitor for the
3000/300L (at least they wouldn't admit it to me on the phone).

At dollars per SPECint92, rounded to nearest $.25:

DEC3k300L  189.50
DEC3k300   196.25
SGI IndySC 258.25
SGI IndR4  312.50
SGI IndyPC 314.25
SS10/30    362.00
HP 715/50  420.50

So the SS10 isn't as expensive as the HP in $/SPECint.  But note that
the HP does much better on FP:

$/SPECfp92

DEC3k300L  136.75
DEC3k300   142.00
HP 715/50  214.25
SGI IndySC 267.25
SGI IndR4  298.50
SS10/30    302.50
SGI IndyPC 314.25

Once again, the Sun is next to last.  If you want integer performance,
there are five machines that beat an SS10, and four of them are
cheaper (note that the more expensive one, the SGI Indigo, has better
graphics).  For FP performance, five of the machines again beat the
SS10, and the one that doesn't is $5,000 cheaper and doesn't lose by
much (looking at price/performance for both statements).  Sun comes in
last on price/performance for those machines listing SPECmark89 scores
(Sun: 279.23, SGI Indigo 256.41, HP 226.08), although those are not as
reliable as the separate integer and fp benchmarks.

As another measure of aggregate price/performance, if you add the
$/SPECfp and $/SPECint (which is mostly meaningless), you see that Sun
is bringing up the rear:

DEC3k300L  326.25
DEC3k300   338.25
SGI IndySC 525.50
SGI IndR4  611.00
SGI IndyPC 628.50
HP 715/50  634.75
SS10/30    665.00

On performance, Sun loses (DEC and SGI have systems that outperform,
and HP has better FP performance for the same price).

On price, Sun loses to DEC, HP, and DEC; and SGI has a cheaper system
for near the performance.

On price/performance, Sun comes in last on aggregate, and next to last
on each of the individual tests (different machines were last for
different tests).

