ejones@desire.wright.edu (Ed Jones) writes:
>I have several unix boxes, DEC3400s and DECstation 5200s, that I want
>to backup the file systems to an Exabyte 8mm drive on the DECstation.
>I want to use tar rather than dump. So, how do I do a backup of file systems
>on the Alpha to a tape drive on the DECstation using tar? Thanks
>for the help.

	Get gnu tar (on various FTP servers) and use it combined with
find.

	gnu tar's nice since you can specify a list of input files
to take. It also can talk to rmt(8) on remote machines, just like
rdump/rrestore. A trick I use for doing incrementals is to build a
file list using find -newer to get a list of files since the last
dump. I then prepend that to itself and use it as the dump index.
My backup script is appended below - it could be fancier but it
works. I use rmtwrite instead of gnu tar's built in rmt code because
I'm having it talk to a tape server on a different port, rather
than invoking rmt(8) via rsh.

mjr.
-----
#!/bin/sh
BIN=/usr/local/etc/backup

LOGS=/logs/backup
FEXT=`date +%m.%d.%y`
TAPE=hilo.tis.com:tape
LAST=$LOGS/lastdump

#we build a file containing a list of files to copy out.
#the first thing in that file is itself. heh.
rm -f $LOGS/files.$FEXT
echo $LOGS/files.$FEXT > $LOGS/files.$FEXT

# build the list
# default is to do everything that changed since yesterday
# if given a parameter, store everything (level 0 dump)
rm -f $LOGS/back.$$
if [ $# -eq 1 ] ; then
        find / -print >> $LOGS/files.$FEXT 2> $LOGS/back.$$
else
        find / -newer $LAST -print >> $LOGS/files.$FEXT 2> $LOGS/back.$$
fi

# dump the files now
$BIN/pdtar -D -c -v -f - -T $LOGS/files.$FEXT | \
        $BIN/rmtwrite -b 20480 -f $TAPE -p 7777 2> $LOGS/back.$$
if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
        (
        echo "dump failed!"
        cat $LOGS/back.$$
        ) | /usr/ucb/mail -s dump root
        exit 1
fi

date > $LAST
compress -f $LOGS/files.$FEXT
rm -f $LOGS/back.$$
exit 0
