[ ... several followups about gnu tar...]
One small thing to remember is that tar reads and writes the files it copies
byte by byte (or buffer by buffer). This means that:
1. tar can't cope with special files (devices and the like)
2. tar copies files with holes as files with the holes filled up with 0 bytes.

This makes tar (IMHO) unsuitable for backup. Use dump instead (rdump can write
to a tapeunit on another host).

With respect to 2, imagine one user makes a file by creating it, seeking to
some very large position, and writing only one byte there. If you backup
that file with tar, I guess that after writing several tapes full you'll
know what I mean. I know it was that hard to explain that tar is not a backup
program at at least one site, where a user actually did this. When there were
many more tapes written than possibly could have been on the disk, the 
system administrator started to understand...

--maarten

