that are labeled (in the announcement materials I have) as NONDISCOUNTABLE,
i.e., the prices are incommensurate with the others ... which has been
missed by most journalists as well, as it's easy to do :-)
As a gross estimate, try adding
25% back onto the DEC prices ... or adding 25% to the $/SPEC #s... to
et equivalent list price from which 20% might be discounted.

Of course, there are other differences (like 2D/3D, audio, ISDN, SCSI
speeds, bus-slots/expandability, graphics upgradeability, bundled software,
camera/video, etc) 
and tweaks on performance-applicability of the SPEC #s (128K-256K
of direct-mapped cache or equivalent does pretty well by SPEC; for larger
codes & running OSs, those with larger caches do a bit better than SPEC would
usually predict (since 512KB & up doesn't really help SPEC #s much,
but helps OS's & other larger things. 
Finally, of course the introduction date also matters ....
