maarten@fwi.uva.nl (Maarten Carels) writes:
>[ ... several followups about gnu tar...]
>One small thing to remember is that tar reads and writes the files it copies
>byte by byte (or buffer by buffer). This means that:
>1. tar can't cope with special files (devices and the like)

Standard tar can't.  GNU Tar can, and has ever since it was John Gilmore's
"pdtar" way back in the late 1980s. 

>2. tar copies files with holes as files with the holes filled up with 0 bytes.

More recent GNU Tar releases support the 'sparse' option for making some
effort to handle this.  But you are right that in general there's no way to
make an *exact* copy of a holey file with the holes in the same places as the
original without going behind the back of the filesystem code.  Whether
this is a big problem for you, you'll have to decide for yourself.

Another, more serious (IMHO) problem with tar-based backups is that they
reset the access time on all the files that get backed up, which makes it
substantially more difficult to determine when files have been left unused
for a long time (and thus are likely candidates for permanent removal to
tape etc.)
--
