In article <230lsm$d9l@sol.TIS.COM> mjr@tis.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes:

>
>	gnu tar's nice since you can specify a list of input files
>to take. It also can talk to rmt(8) on remote machines, just like
>rdump/rrestore. A trick I use for doing incrementals is to build a
>file list using find -newer to get a list of files since the last
>dump.

Note that just saving newer files won't save them in such a way that
a restore can correctly rebuild renamed directories and their contents.
GNUtar version 1.10 and up have a "listed-incremental" option that
keeps a file containing the timestamp of the previous run and a list
of all directories and their corresponding device and inode number.
Using this option you can be sure that all contents of renamed
and new directories are stored in an incremental.

