Note that GNUtar solves both of these problems and a couple of others
that you didn't mention that would apply to other tar versions.
3. It can make sensible incrementals, catching renamed directories
(Version 1.10 and later)
4. It can restore incrementals with an option of deleting files that
did not exist at the time the incremental was taken (important if
you are trying to restore the machine to a particular state or if
you have several incrementals taken over a period of time).

The remaining problem is the lack of a decent manual describing how
and why to use the new features, but there are some sample scripts
included with the distribution.

