Lines: 68
Nntp-Posting-Host: godel.une.edu.au

DEC do not supply DMS with OSF so I thought I would do some mutilations
to (mostly) the startup scripts to allow /usr to mounted across the net.
Here are the steps I took:

On all machines.

 1. /var needs to be its own file system on each machine. Currently OSF
    has /var pointing to /usr/var. We need to do it the other way
    round. So /usr/var should be a soft link to ../var

    # ls -ld /usr/var /var
    lrwxr-xr-x   1 root     bin            6 Jul 08 11:51 /usr/var -> ../var
    drwxr-xr-x  14 root     system       512 Feb 06 01:38 /var

On the server machine:

 2. edit /etc/exports to contain the line:

    /usr -root=0 client [client ....]

On the client machine(s):

 3. Edit /etc/fstab to contain the line:

    /usr@server     /usr    nfs rw,bg 0 0

 4. Edit /sbin/rc2 and /sbin/rc3 and comment out the lines:

    # Just exit if /usr not mounted.
    if [ ! -d "/usr/sbin" ]
    then
           exit
    fi
 
 5. Move /sbin/rc3.d/S20nfsmount to /sbin/rc3.d/S02nfsmount. This will
    mount the /usr partition just after the network initialization and
    before other rc3 scripts require /usr files.

Now reboot your client machines. The old /usr partition on the client
can be used for something else.

Problems you may encounter are:

 1. This will only work for a simple network. If your nfs mounts
    require routed (or gated) to find the server, you can't do this. You
    somehow need to start routed before you mount /usr. Problem is that
    routed is in /usr/sbin and requires shared libs. So just copying
    routed to /sbin is not useful. 

 2. When it comes to upgrade time, the situation becomes a little
    complicated. The way I would go about it is to first upgrade the
    server. Then either connect a scratch disk to the clients, do an
    install with /usr on the scratch disk and then follow the above
    steps. Or you could just copy the new files over from the server to
    the client local areas and rebuild a kernel for the clients. I have
    not tested these methods.

 3. DEC probably won't talk to you if you have a problem with all
    this.

Hope someone finds this useful.

BTW, how can I get a statically linked routed?

--
