Posted to c.u.u to hit the Deccies.

I know disks are getting cheap, and really big disks are getting really
cheap, but...

I have a DEC 3000/500, 96MB's of RAM, 256MB Swap partition.  OSF/1 V1.2

Just me alone, with 10-15 xterms, rlogins, clock, zmail, 2 HX displays, I
can't run a compile 99% of the time with -0, because I'm out of swap
space (the infamous less then 10% swap free message).

Looking through ps guax, I see Xterms at 8+ MB', MWM at 11+, X at 40, etc
etc.  

Now I realize that there was going to be some expansion, and more memory
wastage/usage depending on your point-of-view, but this seems extreme.

For example, I have a 5500 with 128MB's RAM serving a 20 node X terminal
lab, plus about 10 more faculty x terminals, PC's workstations, and it
has 256MB's of swap as well.  We run about 40 users at a time, most of the
load is X development, emacs, compiles, etc.  We have very rarely ever run
out of swap.

I have a 3000/500X with 192MB's of RAM coming.  If I extrapolate out
that number of users, X processes, etc. to the 500x, I'm going to need
2+GB's of swap.  Outrageous.  

Hell, unless I close most of my windows, I can't compile xv3.00a with
full optimization, as the compiler hits something like 70MB's of VM space.

I love the speed of this baby, but there are aspects that disappoint me.

Perhaps it's an OSF shlib implementation issue?  Or maybe the page/swap
area page reservation scheme?

In comparison (not to start a flame war or anything), I went to an xterminal
(DEC VXT2000, booted off an infoserver), and connected the xdm session to
an HP 9000/730, with 64MB's ram, 105MB's swap.  After 10 xterms, and rlogins,
the HP shows 90+MB's free.

I realize this isn't an apple-apple comparison, the tools used may not be
reporting the same thing.

I would be interested in hearing any thoughts on this, or if somebody can 
straighten me out on what's going on.  For all I know, I just need to
set a kernel param, reconfig it, and the behaviour will change, I'm just
not fully up-to-speed yet on the OSF stuff.

Thanks for your time.
