Well I've had an Alpha 3000/400 w/96M of RAM on my desk for 4 or 5 months now,
first with the default 128M of swap, later increased to about 200M (customizing
your partitions is ridiculously hard on that machine, a single user maintenence
shell without even an 'ls' (echo * anyone?) is pretty horrible!)  I found out
after the fact you can remove /sbin/swapdefault, and you don't "reserve" swap
space for all those VM-hungry apps.  So if that's all you are worried about,
rm'ing that one file will solve it.  The way it works is that you reserve
space to cover the VM needs of a process at startup time, to make sure it won't
have to be killed in the kernel because there's nowhere to swap it to.  Its
usefulness is limited if you are never going to exceed your RAM size plus your
swap space in kernel+apps memory requirements.  With 96M+256M, you'd have to be
doing some really heavy-duty stuff...

However, the HP does the same 'hold' strategy, and it does *not* have such
excessive VM problems.  HP's shared library design, while I could think of
some improvements, is far superior to OSF's, IMHO.  And I've used HP's for well
over a year now, and run some pretty major stuff on one, so I know a lot about
how that system handles huge loads (we're talking multiple hundreds of users
running a dedicated application on a 9000/710 here) and its VM requirements.

I'd advise against anyone without lots of disk (or who wants lots of real
users) trying to run an Alpha without rm'ing /etc/swapdefault, the VM design
in OSF is just so incredibly poor wasting that disk just to reserve space does
not make sense.

