Both, more of the latter. The first one asks for a lot of stuff that it
virtually never uses, the second one eagerly preallocates stuff. We've
changed over to "lazy" swap allocation (which is mostly what SGI and HP
seem to be using anyway). That's described in the system administration
manual (basically, delete /sbin/swapdefault after you've added the necessary
description of your swap areas to /etc/fstab). Some of these things will
also improve with the newer releases of the OS (such as the upcoming V1.3
which will tame the compilers memory hunger quite a bit).

