In article <GRUNWALD.93Jul15185451@frob.cs.colorado.edu>, grunwald@frob.cs.colorado.edu (Dirk Grunwald) writes:
|> 
|> 
|> I need to know how DCE/Posix threads access per-pthread information.
|> On our KSR-1, there is a compiler directive '__private' that lets me
|> indicate a copy of that variable should be allocated for each pthread
|> that is created. There is no such directive on our DEC AXP 3000/400.
|> 
|> In the DCE environment, I'm beginning to suspect that you must depend
|> on the intial argument value passed into the thread. Is this the case?
|> Any examples would be helpful.

Sorry I don't have an example to hand, but you should look at the routines
pthread_keycreate(), pthread_setspecific and pthread_getspecific(). These
routines allow "per thread global" information to be stored and retrieved. 
Basically you create a key (which is shared by all threads) and then associate
specific data with it on a per-thread basis.  So you effectively have a key which
"means the same thing" to each thread, but which has a different value for each
thread.  This seems pretty much the semantics of __private you describe above.

|> Thanks,
|> Dirk Grunwald                          Asst. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder

I haven't used this, so it is just my understanding of the documentation, but I
hope it helps.

