> I found out after the fact you can remove /sbin/swapdefault, and you
> don't "reserve" swap space for all those VM-hungry apps.  So if that's
> all you are worried about, rm'ing that one file will solve it.

One warning about using the "lazy" swap allocation method: When swap
is exhausted using this method, process requests for additional memory
fail, and these processes begin to die.  Unfortunately it appears that
various essential root processes in OSF on the DEC 3000 are constantly
making calls for new memory.  So when a user process exhausts VM, the
OS begins to kick off its processes one by one: a nfsd here, an inetd
there, a crond...  In our configuration, a DEC 3000 model 500, with
180 MB of RAM and 640 MB of swap we were able to bomb the operating
system almost 100% of the time by running several large user programs
simultaneously.  After crashing the machine a few days in a row, we
went back to the greedy swap algorithm.

Lincoln

